Subject: devtools: remove the protocol-level tells of an attached CDP client

Almost everyone who runs this browser drives it with Playwright or Puppeteer
over the DevTools protocol, so the differences between an idle Apostate and an
automated one are not an edge case, they are the normal case. Patch 0003 removed
the launch switches that set navigator.webdriver, which was the loud half. The
quiet half is that attaching a client changes how expensive page script is, and
that a remote debugging port answers a page that asks.

Measured on the shipped macos-arm64 build of this series, four launch modes:
no client at all; a client attached with Runtime.enable, which is what both
drivers send; a client attached without it; and a client over
--remote-debugging-pipe. Each number below is a ratio taken inside one page in
one launch, so a detector needs no baseline for the machine it is running on and
no second visit to compare against.

    try { throw 1 } catch {}, cost at call depth 240 over cost at depth 2
        no client                  1.0
        session, no Runtime.enable 1.0
        Runtime.enable            26.5

    console.log(''), same depth ratio
        no client                  1.0
        session, no Runtime.enable 1.0
        Runtime.enable            10.8

    console.trace('') over console.log(''), both at depth 60
        no client                  8.0
        session, no Runtime.enable 6.4
        Runtime.enable             1.009

Two causes, both in V8Debugger. Runtime.enable calls
setMaxCallStackSizeToCapture, which asked the isolate to capture stack traces
for uncaught exceptions; V8 then walks the live JS stack on the throwing thread,
so a throw costs more the deeper the page's own call stack is. Separately
captureStackTrace widened the console capture from one frame to two hundred
whenever any session had the Runtime domain enabled, and the walk again happens
in the page's own call. console.trace and console.timeEnd are pinned - always
full, always one frame - which is what makes the ratios self-calibrating: the
page compares a console call whose cost it cannot change against one whose cost
depends on us.

Both now depend on whether a debug delegate is installed, which is Debugger.
enable. DevTools enables that domain when it attaches, so a human debugging
keeps full console stacks and exception stack traces. Neither Playwright nor
Puppeteer enables it in normal operation, so ordinary automation now costs what
an idle browser costs. The point is not that the numbers get smaller, it is that
every state this browser can be in is a state stock Chrome can also be in: with
no delegate it behaves exactly like Chrome with nothing attached, and with one
exactly like Chrome with DevTools open. Upstream's own precondition is kept, so
the uncaught-exception request only ever narrows.

The console serialisation half was mostly folklore and is worth stating plainly,
because the probe everyone cites does not work. Arguments are stringified
unconditionally, with or without a client, so counting getter invocations
measures nothing; a Proxy is short-circuited to the literal "[object Proxy]"
without touching a trap, in every mode; and value-mirror already declines to
read a property whose own descriptor carries a non-builtin getter, which defeats
the canonical "define a getter on an Error's stack and console.debug it" probe
upstream. Measured, all three are identical attached and unattached, as are
Error.prepareStackTrace, Error.captureStackTrace, Error.stackTraceLimit, stack
line counts and Function.prototype.toString.

What did survive is one step past that guard. When there was no own descriptor,
getErrorProperty fell through to a plain Get, and a plain Get walks the
prototype chain. With stack deleted from an instance and an accessor installed
on Error.prototype, the accessor fired once per console.debug with Runtime
enabled and never without. An accessor on a prototype name went from one read to
three. getErrorProperty now reads the own property or nothing. Every caller
already handles a missing property, and for name the fallback is
GetConstructorName, the value that code prefers anyway, so an error built
normally still serialises the same way.

The remote debugging port is a live tell and does not need a timing channel. A
document in the local address space fetching its own host with mode 'no-cors'
sees the request resolve when --remote-debugging-port is set and reject with
TypeError when it is not, four times out of four. The ledger recorded the
unresolved question as whether Local Network Access blocks the probe before or
after the TCP connect: forcing the document into the public address space with
--ip-address-space-overrides, the probe is blocked and open and closed ports
become indistinguishable by outcome, with no timing separation above the noise
after the first warm-up request. So the answer is that LNA does defend a
public-origin detector at this revision, and the reach that remains is documents
in the local and private address spaces and any configuration with those checks
turned off. That is enough to fix, because it is reachable and because the fix
is cheap.

The endpoint now declines anything a document sent, and declines it by closing
the connection rather than answering. Answering at all is the leak: any HTTP
response, including an error, resolves a no-cors fetch. A closed connection
reaches the page as the same generic failure a refused connection produces. The
discriminators are Origin and Sec-Fetch-Site, both forbidden header names that
page script cannot set or clear, and the rule is built so that every client that
is not a document still works: Puppeteer and Playwright send neither header, the
front-end this endpoint serves sends this endpoint's own origin, and a
chrome:// or devtools:// front-end sends a scheme no page can have. What remains
is that the kernel still completes the TCP handshake before we hang up, so an
open port and a refused one differ by one loopback round trip. Measured, that
difference sits inside the spread of repeated probes to a port that was never
open, and performance.now is clamped to a coarser resolution than the difference
in a page that is not cross-origin isolated, but it is not zero and is reported
rather than claimed away.

Last, performance.memory and console.memory had no emitter. Both read V8's heap
statistics through the same MemoryInfo, and V8 sizes its heap from installed
memory: the old generation is that figure halved and then clamped to 4 GiB. On a
host with 8 GiB or more the result saturates, which is why a 36 GiB host reports
the same jsHeapSizeLimit for every profile in the current pool - 4395630592
bytes read precisely, which is what a site-locked page gets, and 3760000000
after Blink's bucketing elsewhere - and why the problem looks like it is not
there. It is not there on this host. It is there on the machines this browser
actually runs on: a 2 or 4 GiB VM emits a limit no device with the claimed
memory would emit, contradicting navigator.deviceMemory, and nothing in the tree
owned that readout.

gin now hands V8 the profile's figure instead of the host's, clamped to the
host, so the arithmetic stays V8's own and capacity is only ever reduced,
matching the clamp in patches 0004 and 0006. The reported limit is therefore one
the heap genuinely honours, which is why this is a configuration change rather
than an accessor override. A profile that claims less memory than the host now
really gets that device's heap ceiling. That is the intended consequence and not
a side effect, and it is the reason the value is worth changing at all.

Measured across all three attach states, the limit does not move, before or
after this patch. usedJSHeapSize and totalJSHeapSize do rise when a client
attaches, because the inspector really does allocate in the renderer's heap.
Bucketing hides it - read bucketized, all three states report the first bucket
for both - but a site-locked page reads precisely, and there the growth is
visible. That cannot be removed without lying about the heap, and unlike the
ratios above it needs an absolute expectation for a page whose own content sets
the baseline, so it is reported rather than patched.

One thing was investigated and deliberately left alone. Runtime.enable also sets
ForceMainWorldInitialization, which gives a script context to frames that would
not otherwise get one. Across forty sandboxed iframes this moved window.length
not at all, left contentDocument unreadable as expected, and changed the
used-heap delta by about five per cent, which is inside the spread of the
measurement and needs --enable-precise-memory-info to see at all, a switch a
page cannot set. Removing the setting would cost a driver the ability to
evaluate in those frames, which is a real feature, for no measured gain.

Ledger: automation.remote-debugging-port-open, cdp.session-attach-presence,
cdp.console-argument-serialization, cdp.runtime-enable-force-main-world,
introspection.console-memory.

